      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t like that we have to learn the generic word \"File\" as a new concept as distinct from SourceFile and OutputFile with the implication that what makes it different is that it\u0027s relative to a very specific dir.\n\nWhat is the *concept* of a File, not what is its internal shape?\n\nBazel\u0027s File, at the top level, doesn\u0027t need to carry the weight of saying \"a file is *a file* and it\u0027s *relative to this specific path*\" as part of its public contract, seemingly because it clearly defines that a File\u0027s `dirname` is always execroot relative.\n\nCan we do the same?\n\ni.e. a File is conceptually defined as *a file*, not \"a path relative to foo bar\", and the fact it has to be `root_build_dir` relative is instead moved as part of the contract of using it?\n\nHence we always construct a File using the `root_build_dir` relative path, and the `dirname` of a File when used in a build rule is always `root_build_dir` relative?\n\nIf it doesn\u0027t contravene Rust doc commenting style, I also want to see the comment distinguishing File from GN\u0027s SourceFile and OutputFile as the next line right after the opening line for the same reason.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 25,
